From: g2code33 <269358183+g2code33@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 16:56:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Replace native embedded browser webview with a plain iframe
 (fixes full-screen overlay bug)

The in-panel mini-browser used to mount a native child webview via Tauri's
embed_website/wry command, sized and positioned every frame by reading the
container's getBoundingClientRect() and pushing bounds over to Rust with
navigate_website/webview_back/webview_forward/webview_reload/destroy_website.

On Linux (WebKitGTK), that native webview is packed into windowing/GTK
containers that do not respect the bounds passed to it, so it would
intermittently stretch to fill the entire application window instead of
staying inside the browser panel -- a full-screen overlay that blocked the
rest of the UI until the tab was closed.

Fix: render the mini-browser as a plain <iframe>, the same approach already
used for the web (non-Tauri) build. An iframe is an ordinary DOM element; it
is sized with normal CSS/flexbox and cannot escape its parent container the
way a separate native window could -- eliminating the whole class of bug.

Also switches the default in-panel search engine from Google to DuckDuckGo
on desktop (it already defaulted to DuckDuckGo on web). Google sends
X-Frame-Options/CSP headers that refuse to render inside an <iframe> at all,
so searching Google from the panel would just show a blank frame. DuckDuckGo,
like the existing quick-reference links (Wikipedia, PubMed, PubChem,
DailyMed), allows being framed. Sites that still refuse to be framed (e.g.
Google Search results themselves, YouTube, banking, many SPAs) fall back to
the existing 'Open current page in external window' button, which opens the
system browser instead.

The native embed_website/hide_website/destroy_website/navigate_website/
webview_back/webview_forward/webview_reload Tauri commands in
src-tauri/src/main.rs are left registered but are no longer invoked from the
frontend; removing them from the Rust side is left for a follow-up since a
Rust toolchain was not available to compile/verify a change there.

Validated: tsc --noEmit clean, full vitest suite (816/816) passes, eslint
clean (no new warnings -- actually removes one pre-existing exhaustive-deps
warning), and 'npm run build' succeeds.

Co-authored-by: arena-agent <297053741+arena-agent@users.noreply.github.com>
